// ecosystem.nova — Nova stdlib wrapper for MAP 11/12/13 (Web, DB, GUI).
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// IMPORTANT — why this file is flat modules, not nested modules-with-classes:
//
// The originally-requested shape was:
//   module Nova { module Web { class Server { @Native("...") function listen() } } }
//
// Tracing the actual IRBuilder.cpp shipped in this project turned up two
// real gaps that shape would hit:
//
//   1. Class methods never reach the @Native check at all. lowerClass()
//      calls lowerFunction() directly for every method (IRBuilder.cpp,
//      lowerClass) — it never routes through lowerAnnotated(), which is
//      the ONLY place @Native is recognized. A method written as
//      `class Server { @Native("x") function listen() }` would silently
//      fall back to lowering `listen`'s (empty) body instead of binding to
//      the native symbol.
//   2. lowerAnnotated() calls plain lowerFunction(*f) with no forced name,
//      so an @Native function declared *inside* a module loses that
//      module's name prefix (IRBuilder.hpp's lowerFunction(fn, forcedName)
//      overload — the forcedName path — is only used by lowerModuleDecl's
//      bare-FunctionDecl branch and lowerClass, neither of which apply to
//      an annotated function).
//
// Rather than ship syntax that parses but silently does the wrong thing at
// IR-lowering time, this file uses the ONE pattern that is actually proven
// correct against the real IRBuilder.cpp right now: a top-level
// `@Native("...") function name(...)` directly inside a single (non-nested)
// `module`. That path is exactly `lowerModuleDecl`'s `else lowerStmt(*s)`
// branch -> `lowerAnnotated` -> `lowerNativeBoundFunction`, which is fully
// implemented and wired end to end.
//
// Once the two gaps above are patched (class-member @Native support, and
// module-name-prefixing for annotated functions), this file's contents can
// be re-nested into `module Nova { module Web { class Server { ... } } }`
// with zero change to nova_ecosystem.cpp — the native symbol names on each
// @Native(...) don't change, only how Nova source code organizes them.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ── MAP 11: Web ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
module NovaWeb:
    // Starts a real TCP listener on `port`, accepts exactly one connection,
    // serves a minimal real HTTP/1.1 response, and returns a status string
    // describing what happened (see nova_ecosystem.cpp: nova_web_server_listen
    // for the actual POSIX/Winsock socket implementation this call reaches).
    //
    // For a persistent server, call this in a loop from a `thread`:
    //   thread WebServer:
    //       while true:
    //           result = NovaWeb.listen(8080)
    //           Nova.show(result)
    @Native("nova_web_server_listen")
    function listen(port):
        return null

// ── MAP 12: Database (SQLite3) ──────────────────────────────────────────
module NovaDB:
    // Opens (or creates) a SQLite3 database file at `path` and returns an
    // opaque integer handle (or an "error: ..." string on failure — check
    // with a Nova-level type/prefix check, since NovaValue stays untyped
    // at this boundary; a richer Result<Int, String> wrapper is a natural
    // follow-up once Nova's own error-propagation syntax is wired through
    // this same @Native path).
    @Native("nova_db_sqlite_open")
    function open(path):
        return null

    // Executes `query` against the database identified by `handle`.
    // Returns either the query's row output (tab-separated "col=value"
    // lines, one per row) or an "ok: N row(s) affected" summary for
    // statements with no result set, or an "error: ..." string.
    @Native("nova_db_sqlite_exec")
    function exec(handle, query):
        return null

    // Closes a handle opened via NovaDB.open. Returns true/false.
    @Native("nova_db_sqlite_close")
    function close(handle):
        return null

// ── MAP 13: GUI (Xlib on Linux/POSIX, Win32 on Windows) ─────────────────
module NovaGUI:
    // Opens a real, visible native window titled `title` at `width` x
    // `height`, and blocks until the user closes it (window-manager close
    // button, or Escape). Returns "ok: window closed" when it returns.
    //
    // Because this call blocks for the lifetime of the window, a program
    // that also needs to do other work while the window is open should run
    // it on its own thread:
    //   thread MainWindow:
    //       NovaGUI.createWindow("Nova App", 800, 600)
    @Native("nova_gui_window_create")
    function createWindow(title, width, height):
        return null


// ── Example program exercising all three domains ────────────────────────
// (Not itself part of the stdlib wrapper above — shows the intended
// call-site ergonomics once this file is imported.)
//
// import NovaWeb
// import NovaDB
// import NovaGUI
//
// dbHandle = NovaDB.open("app.sqlite3")
// Nova.show(NovaDB.exec(dbHandle, "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users(id INTEGER, name TEXT)"))
// Nova.show(NovaDB.exec(dbHandle, "INSERT INTO users VALUES (1, 'Javed')"))
// Nova.show(NovaDB.exec(dbHandle, "SELECT * FROM users"))
// NovaDB.close(dbHandle)
//
// thread WebServer:
//     Nova.show(NovaWeb.listen(8080))
//
// NovaGUI.createWindow("Nova Ecosystem Demo", 800, 600)
